        "content": "**Product Overview:**\nBenzi is an AI coding agent that emphasizes reading and understanding codebases rather than guessing. The tagline describes it as \"an AI coding agent that doesn't guess — it reads.\"\n\n**Core Approach:**\nThe product distinguishes itself from competitors by compiling codebases into a resolved map before analysis. Instead of dumping files into a context window, Benzi creates a structured map containing calls, data flow, and references, then navigates it with real tools.\n\n**Key Features:**\n- Multi-language support covering Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, Java, C#, C++, C, Go, Rust, and Ruby\n- Three-tier truth system with proven edges, candidate edges, and runtime-observed edges\n- Runtime tracer that hooks calls to capture real argument values and returns\n- Model-agnostic design supporting Anthropic, OpenAI, or compatible APIs\n- Persistent memory for per-repo facts\n- Dual-engine for both code and markup (HTML/CSS/DOM-JS)\n\n**Technical Architecture:**\nThe system uses a tree-sitter compiler to produce resolved maps, then runs an agent loop for navigation and editing. It indexes call flow and data flow separately, joining them at call sites. Tools include skim, backflow, forwardflow, and trace_path functions.\n\n**Comparison to Alternatives:**\nThe page contrasts Benzi with Claude Code (which uses grep), Cursor (embeddings), and Aider (tree-sitter signatures), noting that Benzi indexes \"the full resolved structure.\"\n\n**Safety Features:**\nEdits are syntax-gated using real language parsers, with auto-revert for broken parses. The system reports blast radius for successful edits.\n\n**Availability:**\nUsers can try it online at benzi.fly.dev, install a VS Code extension, or view benchmarks. The project is open-source on GitHub."
