        "content": "Based on the GitHub repository, here's the information about LongHorizon-Harness:\n\n**What It Does**\nLongHorizon-Harness is an execution, state-management, and result-verification system designed for long-horizon computer-use tasks. It enables AI agents to \"operate autonomously in real computer environments for extended periods and continuously move complex tasks forward.\" The system supports both GUI desktop applications and command-line workflows.\n\n**Key Features**\nThe system uses a three-role architecture:\n- **Manager** - Maintains original goals and verified progress\n- **Executor** - Handles specific subtasks with fresh context\n- **Auditor** - Independently inspects files, interfaces, and logs\n\nOther capabilities include fresh-context execution, durable verified state, independent auditing, and recoverable progress. It supports Claude Code and Codex backends, with compatibility for various models through an OpenAI-compatible endpoint.\n\n**Performance**\nAccording to the README, benchmarks show significant improvements:\n- WeaveBench GUI+CLI completion: ~50% → ~80%\n- OSWorld 2.0 full desktop-task completion: 3× improvement\n- Terminal-Bench 2.1 success: 69.7% → 77.2%\n\n**Installation & Usage**\nThe system can be installed via `uv tool install lh-harness` or `pip install lh-harness`. Users initialize projects with `lh-harness init` and run tasks with `lh-harness run --task \"task description\"`. A web dashboard provides visibility into execution progress.\n\n**Project Details**\nThe project is public on GitHub under AMAP-ML/LongHorizon-Harness, uses an MIT License, and requires Python 3.10 or later. It was recognized as \"#1 on the Hugging Face Daily Papers weekly ranking\" on August 6, 2026."
